good afternoon my name is harry attredge  chairman this year of the dwight h terry  lectureship committee and i'm delighted  to welcome you to this very special  celebration  the terry lectureship was established  about a century ago  by a gift from dwight harrington terry  of bridgeport connecticut to endow a  series of lectures on religion and its  application to human welfare in the  light of scientific knowledge and  philosophical insights  these lectures have produced a long  series of distinguished books published  by yale university press and to  celebrate that longer distinguished  history  we've decided this year to have a very  special celebration with five  distinguished lecturers  at a panel which is going to be chaired  by margaret warner  newly on the yale corporation  so we welcome you to this this series  this is an interactive event and if you  have questions or issues that would you  would like to raise with our lecturers  you may do so through the yale website  www.yale.edu slash terry lecture  the format today will be  a series of lectures followed by  questions and answers we will take a  brief break at 3 30 and be back for the  second lecture of the afternoon at 4. we  will conclude promptly  in time for us to leave this room by 5  30 this evening for reception upstairs  in room 208. we need to leave because  there is a special screening in this  room this evening and the room has to be  cleared at that time  at this point to introduce our first  lecturer i'd like to call on my  colleague  in religious studies and sociology phil  gorsky who works on the very timely  issue of religion and politics  thank you very much it's a great  pleasure and honor for me to be able to  introduce  robert whatnow who is professor of  sociology and chair of the sociology  department at princeton university  and i think it would be fair to say that  he is the most prominent sociologist of  religion working in the united states  today  i'd add that he's also one of the most  prolific  he's written dozens of books edited  dozens more and written scores and  scores of articles in fact one of my  colleagues once equipped to me that she  was going to have to stop reading all  the things that bob was writing so that  she could get some of her own writing  done  i wouldn't recommend that because i  think there are a few  people in the united states today who  have their pulse  on  american  religious life  the way that professor what now does  he's worked on a wide number of topics  including relations between evangelicals  and liberals  popular religion and spirituality  the role of arts and music  in contemporary christianity just to  name a few and today he is going to  start us off in our dialogue about  religion and science  well thank you i appreciate the  opportunity to be here today  it's impressive when you think about it  that the terry lectures began about 100  years ago and  that the conflict that we think about  between religion and science is still as  important as it was  way back then  today to start us off i want to take a  rather  unorthodox approach to this topic  i'm going to focus on those folks who  see no conflict  between religion and science  and just to give you a brief preview of  where i'm going with this i'm going to  spend the first part of my  lecture trying to explain some of the  reasons why there should be  conflict between religion and science  and then discuss some of the reasons why  there isn't more conflict than there is  so as has been  announced my topic is no contradictions  here  science religion and the culture of all  reasonable possibilities  in seeking perspective on recent  controversies about religion and science  one can hardly do better than the wisdom  provided by that bible of guidance and  revelation for academics the new york  times  in its coverage of litigation about the  teaching of intelligent design  and of school board decisions about  evolution  and of policy debates about stem cell  research and genetic engineering the  nation's favorite arbiter of elite  opinion  has documented beyond doubt that  questions about the respective roles of  science and religion remain of great  public interest  it is this public interest  or more precisely the cultural role  of these controversies on which i wish  to focus  what fascinates me about the new york  times coverage is not its reportage or  even its editorials  but the letters it chooses to print from  its readers  unsurprisingly many of the letters speak  in favor of science  while others side clearly with religion  the former variously associate science  with hard evidence discovery and  progressive thinking  in contrast with the ludicrous  disheartening  and even dangerous claims of religious  ideologues  the latter question whether scientific  facts are as firmly established as  scientists claim and worry about the  moral implications of science  a few accuse scientists of promoting an  agenda of unfettered atheism  but far more interesting are the letters  that seek to reconcile science and  religion  for example a reader from minneapolis  writes  in my view there is no contradiction  between evolution and religion  one explains how and the other explains  why  a scientist in los angeles observes  scientists science is a tool for  furthering human understanding of the  natural world  and as such can co-exist with religion  a reader in new jersey elaborates  science is about the natural material  world  religion is about the supernatural and  spiritual  science operates mainly with numbers  religion with words  and this reader concludes in short they  are separate but equal  well these readers are the voices of  reason  the defenders of a middle ground  threatened as much by the simplistic  proponents of science as by the radical  claims of religious extremists  theirs is a both and rather than an  either or world  and they are in distinguished company  they stand with albert einstein