example of serious scholarship since his  book is candidly autobiographical and  impressionistic  yet it is worth noting that his work has  been warmly embraced in the social  science community  a more thoroughly academic example can  be found in the work of sociologist amy  binder whose book contentious curricula  examines creationist controversies  binder argues that creationists are  cleverer than the naive reader may have  supposed  leaders frame a technical sociological  term here leader's frame appeals in  religious terms when speaking to church  groups but downplay this rhetoric when  talking to the media or in the courts  they strategize and mobilize against  school boards  rather than against larger governing  bodies because it takes fewer resources  to do so  it helps she says when they win  elections to these boards it hurts when  school administrators muscle  creationists decide with assertions  about separation of church and state  it's understandable that scholars focus  on what they perceive as deviant  behavior  the very idea of a few citizens in out  of the way places like kansas or dover  pennsylvania promoting strange ideas  since journalists and social scientists  scurrying  to study this quaint phenomenon  it is as if the amish suddenly ran a  candidate for president  what would the world be like if these  creationists whom their own neighbors  describe as the laughingstock of the  world  succeeded in eliminating science from  the curriculum  perhaps there is something to be learned  here about the peculiarities of american  culture  just as there is from studying serial  killers  if the goal is to understand american  culture it would nevertheless seem  strange to focus on the periphery and  neglect the center  the both and position expressed in  thoughtful letters to the editor is held  by a large share of the american public  in opinion polls this middle ground is  favored by a majority or large minority  of the public depending on the wording  of specific questions  for instance a cbs news survey conducted  in 2005 found that 67 percent of the  public believes it is possible to  believe in both god and creation uh  sorry both god and evolution while only  29 thought this was not possible  the sheer reasonableness of the both and  approach though  necessarily raises two interesting  puzzles  first why is it that science and  religion appear to be in conflict at all  is it that the antagonists who  perpetrate these struggles are less  reasonable than everybody else  is it perhaps true that they are simply  duped by their leaders  if it is so sensible to argue that  science and religion are simply  different  would it not be more likely that  conflict between the two would be  non-existent  pursuing these questions will provide a  way of examining afresh why religion and  science  might not be so easily reconciled even  for reasonable people  once we have considered these  possibilities though a different puzzle  arises  why are the conflicts between science  and religion as infrequent as they are  this question will take us into the  larger terrain of contemporary culture  where we can view the assumptions that  undergird our conviction that reasonable  people can simply resolve their  differences if they bring enough gray  matter to the table  a useful place to begin is the idea  commonly known in sociological theory as  institutional differentiation  the idea of institutional  differentiation simply put  is that the various spheres of social  life  are more distinct and specialized now  than they probably were in earlier times  when societies were smaller and less  complex  for instance max weber argued that one  of the important preconditions for the  rise of modern capitalism was the  separation of paid labor from the  household  this separation of spheres faber  suggested made it possible for labor to  be governed with greater attention to  rewarding productivity than was the case  when workers were